# github-pr

Own every GitHub PR conversation posted on the user's behalf, from either side. The modes:

- `review ready` discovers review requests and groups related ones. Subagents review them in the background while this session triages each PR with the user and hands the approved review to a posting subagent. Where the harness can't run subagents, it dispatches an interactive reviewer per group into a tab instead.
- `review <n>` conducts one review where it is invoked, from the PR's diff with no checkout, triages findings with the user, and posts only after confirmation.
- `respond <n>` drafts the author's replies to the PR's unanswered review comments, walks the user through each, and posts only after confirmation.

A bare `<n>`, or no mode at all, does not pick one. Ask whether to review or respond, suggesting `respond` when the authenticated user is the PR's author and `review` otherwise.

This skill may be invoked directly, by any agent, or repeatedly with `/loop 5m /github-pr review ready` in a Claude session that remains open. Both GitHub scripts below, and the schema of everything posted, are in [references/posting.md](references/posting.md); read it before reading a PR's threads or posting.

## Open with the summary

The PR's summary is how the user tells which PR is in front of them, so every review and response opens with it:

- A tab reviewer that `review ready` dispatched gets it before the review starts: the dispatcher sends `/summarize-github-pr <n>` as the session's first prompt, so don't run it again.
- A `review ready` session working with subagents opens each PR's triage with its board instead, and shows the summary's table in the confirmation, as [references/queue.md](references/queue.md) describes.
- A `respond <n>` session, and a `review <n>` the user started by hand, opens with the summary itself: before reading anything else about the PR, run the `summarize-github-pr` skill and send its output, every line as markdown, as your first message.

## `review ready`: work the queue

Read [references/queue.md](references/queue.md), then run the queue flow there. Do not ask the user to confirm the discovered set: `review ready` is explicit authorization to review the requested queue, including when a loop invoked it.

It runs one of two ways, chosen by what the harness can do:

- **Subagents**, whenever the harness can start subagents that run in the background while you keep working, such as Claude Code's Agent tool or Codex's `spawn_agent`. Subagents review the PRs, and this session holds every triage: as each review lands, it triages that PR with the user, hands the confirmed review to a posting subagent, and moves to the next PR while that one posts. A board shows where each PR stands.
- **Tabs**, only when the harness has no such subagents. This needs a workspace tool the `dispatch` skill supports, Herdr (`HERDR_ENV=1`) or Orca (`ORCA_PANE_KEY` set). Each tab's reviewer triages and posts with the user itself, and this session only launches, advances, and closes them.

With neither, say so and stop. Either way, look across the queue for PRs that belong together before reviewing, such as a stack or two halves of one change, and give each group one reviewer that takes its PRs in order; say how you grouped them and why.

## `review <n>`: conduct the review

Run this inline, in the session that invokes it. First claim the PR, so a `review ready` queue leaves it alone while you work:

```bash
scripts/dispatch.py claim --pr-number <n> [--reviewer <name>]
```

Pass `--reviewer` with the name the prompt gave you when `review ready` sent you this PR in a tab. Started any other way, pass none, and keep the `holder` the result returns, a token that marks the claim as this session's. A needs-judgment result means another reviewer or session has the PR in progress: tell the user which, and go on only if they say so.

Then [review the PR](#review-the-pr). While the reviewer runs, tell the user in a line or two that it is running and what you asked it to focus on; the summary that opened the session already describes the PR. When it returns, [triage with the user](#triage-with-the-user), [post](#post), and [record the pass](#record-the-pass).

When `review ready` sent you this PR in a tab as part of a group, its prompt names the other PRs and why they belong together. Use the earlier ones as context, and don't raise again a finding already posted on one of them. If that prompt named a session to tell when the pass is complete, send it the message the prompt gives once the pass is recorded. Then stop: in a group, the next PR arrives as a new prompt, so don't start it yourself.

## Review the PR

This is the review itself, up to triage. `review <n>` runs it inline, and a `review ready` queue gives it to a subagent, which returns the results below rather than triaging.

A review reads the PR from git's object store rather than a checkout. Start with the head check in [Keep up with the head](#keep-up-with-the-head), which fetches the PR's head and base without touching the working tree and returns `head_sha`, `base_branch`, and `merge_base`.

Read the PR title, author, body, and changed files, and run `scripts/feedback.py <n>` for the review conversation so far: my prior findings in the threads I started, and the open comments of other reviewers (see [Other reviewers' comments](#other-reviewers-comments)).

Invoke the shared `review` skill once per head with a self-contained brief. A queue's reviewer subagent runs it inline, in its own context, rather than starting another subagent: it already is the fresh context the `review` skill asks for, and a second layer would only sit idle holding a copy of the brief. Inline `review <n>` may still give it to a subagent. The brief contains:

- the PR's stated intent and base branch
- the change scope, `git diff <merge_base> <head_sha>`, which is the diff GitHub shows
- that the working tree is not the PR's code, since it may be on any branch: read the PR's code with `git show <head_sha>:<path>` and search it with `git grep <pattern> <head_sha>`, and don't run its tests, which have no checkout to run in
- my unresolved prior findings and their thread comment IDs, to verify against the current code rather than raise again
- the other reviewers' open comments, as context, so it doesn't report what they already raised as if it were new
- an explicit instruction not to report Nits

The shared reviewer returns Blockers and Follow-ups without editing, interaction, a verdict, or posting. Preserve its classifications unless discussion with the user supplies new technical evidence. Then take a stance on the other reviewers' comments yourself, as below, from its findings and your own reading of the code.

On a re-review, each prior finding the reviewer verifies as fixed is a thread to resolve: resolving is how the reviewer confirms a fix holds. A prior finding that still stands gets a reply on its thread instead of a new inline comment.

The results, which a subagent returns as its final message and an inline review carries into triage, are:

- `head_sha`, `base_branch`, and `merge_base` as reviewed
- each finding, with its classification, title, and impact, the anchor it would post at (a path and a RIGHT-side line in the diff, or a short name for the summary body when nothing anchors; see [references/posting.md](references/posting.md)), and the comment it would post, prefixed `[Blocker]` or `[Follow-up]`
- each prior finding, with its thread's root comment ID, as fixed or still standing, and for one still standing, the reply it would post
- each other reviewer's comment it takes a stance on, with its thread's root comment ID or its review or comment ID, its author, the comment's point in a line, the stance with its severity, and the reply it would post
- what the reviewer verified, and anything it couldn't

### Other reviewers' comments

Other reviewers, people and bots alike, may have raised concerns already. Weighing in on them tells the author which ones matter and how much, so the review answers them as well as adding its own. From `feedback.py`, take:

- each unresolved thread started by someone other than me and the PR's author, in which I haven't replied (`replied_by_me` is false)
- each review summary body and conversation comment by another reviewer that is `after_my_latest`, leaving out status and tooling comments that raise no concern about the code, such as a CI or deploy-preview bot's

Judge each one against the code and take one of three stances. The shared `review` skill doesn't do this; its report covers only problems the change introduces, so the stances are yours, informed by what it found:

- **Agree**: the concern holds. The reply gives it my severity, `[Blocker]` or `[Follow-up]`, and adds whatever the review found that strengthens it. When my severity is higher than the comment's own framing, such as a bot's suggestion that would break something, the reply says so: that is how a comment is escalated to a Blocker.
- **Dispute**: the concern doesn't hold. The reply says why, with evidence from the code, plainly and without arguing.
- **No stance**: a Nit, a matter of taste, a point already settled in its thread, or one outside the change's scope. Nothing is posted.

A finding of the review's own that another reviewer already raised becomes an Agree on their comment rather than a new comment of mine.

In triage, each Agree or Dispute is an item like a finding. An included Agree counts toward the GitHub event at its severity, so agreeing that another reviewer's comment is a Blocker requests changes.

### Keep up with the head

The author may push while the review is open, and a review formed against older code can't post. So check the head when the review starts, before triage's first question, and before the final confirmation:

```bash
scripts/dispatch.py fetch --pr-number <n>
```

Run it from any checkout of the repository; under a sandbox that proxies network access, it reaches `api.github.com` and `github.com`. It fetches the PR's current head to a private ref and its base branch to `origin/<base>`, and changes nothing in the working tree. It returns `moved: true`, with `previous_head` and `rewritten`, when the head differs from the last fetch.

At the start, review whatever head it returns. When it moves later, tell the user in a line that the head moved and what the new commits do, then review again before going on: the reviewer gets the same brief plus the commits since `previous_head` (`previous_head..head_sha`, or a diff between the two heads when `rewritten` says it was a force-push) and this pass's results so far with the user's rulings. Inline, invoke the shared `review` skill again; in a `review ready` queue, the PR goes back to its reviewer subagent. Triage only what is new or materially changed. An item the user already ruled on that still stands keeps its ruling, and one the new commits fix drops out; name the dropped ones in triage's count line, such as `1 Follow-up; the hero height one is fixed by 066422b`.

## `respond <n>`: reply as the author

Run this inline, in the session that invokes it; it dispatches nothing. Answers rest on the code as it stands: read it from a checkout of the PR's head when one exists, or else from git after `scripts/dispatch.py fetch --pr-number <n>`, with `git show <head_sha>:<path>`. If the authenticated user is not the PR's author, say so and ask before going on.

Run `scripts/feedback.py <n>` and take every `unanswered` inline thread, whoever started it, and every review summary body and conversation comment that is not my own. Whether a summary or comment was already answered is a judgment the API can't make: list those after `my_latest` as needing replies, and the older ones separately as possibly answered, so triage settles them rather than a timestamp. Bot reviewers are included; say which items are a bot's, so the user can skip them quickly.

Draft one reply for each item, grounded in evidence rather than intent:

- **Fixed**: name the commit that fixes it and what changed, such as `Fixed in 4f1c2e9: the token now expires after an hour.` The commit must already be on the PR; cite it only after reading its diff against the comment.
- **Declined**: the reason, stated plainly and without arguing.
- **Answered**: for a question, the answer from the code.
- **Deferred**: what will happen instead, such as a follow-up ticket, when the caller or the user has said so.

When nothing settles an item (no commit addresses it and no one has said whether it will be fixed), don't invent a stance. Show the user the comment, mark the reply undecided, and let triage settle it.

Triage follows [Triage with the user](#triage-with-the-user), with the drafts as its items: show each reviewer's comment and its draft reply, and `Other` edits the reply. The final confirmation shows every reply that will post and where it lands, and whose review will be re-requested.

When a human reviewer's review stands at changes requested (`changes_requested_by`), re-request their review by default once the replies post, since their request otherwise keeps blocking the merge until they happen to look again. Name them in the confirmation, where the user can drop one or add another human who reviewed. Bots aren't re-requested; their reviews run on their own triggers.

Render the confirmed replies into a batch with no review: `thread_replies` for inline threads, and at most one `issue_comment` that answers review summary bodies and conversation comments by naming or quoting each point it addresses. List every commit a reply cites in `commits`, and the reviewers to re-request in `rerequest`. Never resolve a thread in this mode, even one whose fix has landed: that confirmation is the reviewer's to give.

## Triage with the user

Open triage with a one-line count of the items, such as `2 Blockers, 3 Follow-ups, and 2 replies to Copilot`, then ask about them. In a review, the items are the findings, the replies on my prior findings that still stand, and the Agree and Dispute replies to other reviewers.

Every item gets its own question and its own ruling, but the questions go out together, Blockers first. Asking one item per prompt ("Follow-up 1 of 5", wait, "Follow-up 2 of 5", …) costs the user a round trip per finding and gains nothing, because findings rarely depend on each other. Ask only whether each item should be included; do not ask for an overall verdict yet.

- With a structured question UI, use as few calls as it allows: AskUserQuestion takes four questions per call, so five items take two calls. Offer `Include` and `Exclude`; the UI's own free-text option opens discussion or lets the user rewrite or reclassify the item.
- Without one, number the items and ask `Include?` after each, so a reply like `1y 2n 3y` settles the batch and a fuller answer on any item opens discussion of it.

Hold an item back for a later round only when its question depends on another item's ruling: two findings with one root cause, where excluding or rewriting one changes what the other should say, or a reply whose stance turns on how another item is settled. Ask the item it depends on first, then the dependent one, worded for that ruling. When findings are that entangled, asking them as one item is often simpler.

Keep each question concise: what the item is and its concrete consequence. For a reply to another reviewer, name whose comment it answers and the stance, such as `Agree with Copilot, as a Blocker: the retry loop never exits`. Do not put the full technical body or file coordinates in the question unless the user asks to discuss it. Preserve each ruling as you proceed; when the user opens discussion on one item of a batch, settle it and keep the rulings the rest of the batch already gave.

In a review, derive the GitHub event from the included items: any Blocker, whether my own finding or an Agree with another reviewer, means `REQUEST_CHANGES`; otherwise use `APPROVE`. A Follow-up never changes the event by itself. If nothing remains included, the review is an approval. When the review found nothing to triage, go straight to the confirmation.

After triage, show everything that will post and explicitly ask for confirmation before posting: in a review, the event, the included findings, the replies, and the threads to be resolved. Open the confirmation with the summary's table (number and title, author and URL, ready time and stack) copied from the PR's summary, running `summarize-github-pr` for it if this session hasn't, so the user can see which PR it is posting to. If the user declines, revise or stop as directed. Never infer posting approval from the earlier per-item rulings.

## Post

Render the confirmed content into a batch, with this exact header, so every posted message is attributed:

```markdown
{{ includeTemplate ".chezmoitemplates/posting-attribution.md" . | trim }}
```

In a review, the batch has one inline comment per included finding at its anchor, a finding with no anchor in the body under its name, `thread_replies` for the replies on my still-standing prior findings and on other reviewers' threads, and `resolve` for the verified fixes. Replies to other reviewers' summary bodies and conversation comments, which have no thread, go in the review's body, naming whose point each answers. When the event rests on an Agree at Blocker, the body names that point too, such as `Requesting changes for Copilot's point on the retry loop (see its thread)`, so the review says why even before its replies land.

Set `head_sha` to the head the content was formed against. Run `scripts/post.py --dry-run batch.json` and resolve every problem it reports. If the PR's head has moved, do not post content formed against the stale code. In a review, go back through [Keep up with the head](#keep-up-with-the-head) and confirm again. In `respond`, read the new commits, revise any draft they change (a new commit may fix what a reply was about to decline), and confirm again. Only then run `scripts/post.py batch.json`. If a step fails after part of the batch landed, retry only with the remainder batch the poster prints, never the original. Do not fall back to hand-built `gh` posting if the script fails. Posting is the only external mutation this skill performs, and only after the confirmation above.

A posting subagent from a `review ready` queue follows the same steps with the content it was handed, and changes none of it. Any fix a check asks for, such as moving a finding whose anchor won't post into the body, changes what the user confirmed, so it posts nothing and reports the problem back instead.

## Record the pass

When a review reaches a terminal decision (successfully posted, or the user explicitly decides to finish without posting), record the pass complete:

```bash
scripts/dispatch.py complete --pr-number <n> --head-sha <head_sha> [--reviewer <name> | --holder <token>]
```

with the same `--reviewer` or `--holder` the PR was claimed with. Don't record completion if posting failed or the user has not finished triage, so the queue never treats an unfinished review as done.

## Boundaries

- Do not edit the PR's source in any mode.
- Do not request or report Nits.
- Do not run more than one reviewer for the same PR.
- Do not close a reviewer's tab except through `review ready`'s cleanup step, or when the user separately asks.
- Resolve only threads I started, and only in a review.
